import{_ as t,o as a,c as o,a2 as n}from"./chunks/framework.D_ZOUi6P.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"Code review","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"development/code-review.md","filePath":"development/code-review.md","lastUpdated":1793275217000}'),i={name:"development/code-review.md"};function r(s,e,c,d,l,h){return a(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="code-review" tabindex="-1">Code review <a class="header-anchor" href="#code-review" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Code review&quot;">​</a></h1><p>This page covers the guidelines we use when reviewing changes, whether the reviewer is a human or an AI. To check your own change before opening a PR, read through the checklists below.</p><h2 id="how-ai-review-works" tabindex="-1">How AI review works <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-ai-review-works" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How AI review works&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A collaborator with write access can comment <code>/dd-review</code> on a PR to request an AI code review. The review uses the <code>codereview_guideline.md</code> files in the repository as its instructions:</p><ul><li>The file at the repository root applies to every PR.</li><li>A file in a subdirectory applies only to PRs that change files under that subdirectory.</li><li>The guidelines are read from <code>main</code>, not from the PR, so changes to them take effect after they merge.</li></ul><p>The review action removes <code>AGENTS.md</code> and <code>CLAUDE.md</code> files before the review runs, so each guideline file <strong>MUST</strong> stay self-contained, rather than pointing to other pages. This makes the guideline files one of the few exceptions to <a href="./style-guide#where-documentation-lives">Where documentation lives</a>. To add review guidance for one area of the codebase, add a <code>codereview_guideline.md</code> file in that area&#39;s directory and include it on this page.</p><p>The sections below render the guideline files as they&#39;re written.</p><h2 id="general-guidelines" tabindex="-1">General guidelines <a class="header-anchor" href="#general-guidelines" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;General guidelines&quot;">​</a></h2><p>From <code>codereview_guideline.md</code>:</p><p>You are acting as a reviewer for a proposed code change made by another engineer. Focus on issues that impact correctness, performance, security, maintainability, or developer experience, in roughly that order: correctness comes first, then performance. Flag only actionable issues introduced by the pull request. When you flag an issue, provide a short, direct explanation and cite the affected file and line range. Prioritize severe issues and avoid nit-level comments unless they block understanding of the diff. Do not flag formatting or lint issues that <code>make fmt</code> and <code>make check-all</code> already enforce. After listing findings, produce an overall correctness verdict (&quot;patch is correct&quot; or &quot;patch is incorrect&quot;) with a concise justification. Ensure that file citations and line numbers are exactly correct using the tools available; if they are incorrect your comments will be rejected.</p><h2 id="project-specific-review-checklist" tabindex="-1">Project-specific review checklist <a class="header-anchor" href="#project-specific-review-checklist" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Project-specific review checklist&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Saluki is a Rust toolkit for building observability data planes. Its primary artifact, <code>bin/agent-data-plane</code>, runs alongside the Datadog Agent.</p><h3 id="generic-crates-must-stay-generic" tabindex="-1">Generic crates must stay generic <a class="header-anchor" href="#generic-crates-must-stay-generic" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Generic crates must stay generic&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Crates under <code>lib/saluki-*</code> (including <code>lib/saluki-io</code>) are general-purpose infrastructure. Flag code, comments, or documentation in those crates that refer to the Datadog Agent by name, to internal Datadog codenames or project names, or to Datadog-specific deployment topologies. This rule does not apply to <code>bin/agent-data-plane</code>, <code>lib/datadog-agent/</code>, configuration registry annotations, or known-configs documentation.</p><h3 id="generated-and-vendored-files" tabindex="-1">Generated and vendored files <a class="header-anchor" href="#generated-and-vendored-files" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Generated and vendored files&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Flag hand edits to files marked <code>@generated</code> or <code>DO NOT EDIT</code>; the source of truth must be changed and the file regenerated instead. Flag hand edits to the vendored Datadog schema under <code>lib/datadog-agent/config/schema/core/</code>, which must stay a pristine upstream copy.</p><h3 id="hot-paths-and-allocation" tabindex="-1">Hot paths and allocation <a class="header-anchor" href="#hot-paths-and-allocation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Hot paths and allocation&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Much of Saluki processes high volumes of metrics, logs, and traces. In per-event code paths, flag new heap allocations, clones, or string formatting that could be avoided, unbounded buffering or queues, and logging at any level that scales with event volume. In particular:</p><ul><li>Flag a clone or an owned <code>String</code> where a reference, a <code>&amp;&#39;static str</code>, or a value the code already owns would work, and flag methods that silently clone large data structures.</li><li>Fixed strings stored in events, tags, or contexts should use <code>MetaString::from_static</code> or existing string interning rather than allocating a new <code>String</code> for each event.</li><li>Flag code that collects into a temporary <code>Vec</code> or map only to iterate over it once, when it could iterate directly.</li><li>Flag per-event construction of builders, resolvers, or buffers that could be created once and reused. Regressions that only affect tail latency (p95, p99) still count.</li></ul><h3 id="concurrency-and-component-lifecycle" tabindex="-1">Concurrency and component lifecycle <a class="header-anchor" href="#concurrency-and-component-lifecycle" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Concurrency and component lifecycle&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Components run concurrently on Tokio and have explicit startup and shutdown. Flag blocking calls on async tasks, locks held across <code>.await</code> points, spawned tasks that are never joined or cancelled, and shutdown paths that can drop buffered data or hang. Changes to component lifecycle should have tests that exercise graceful shutdown.</p><ul><li>Flag code that drops messages or updates without telemetry or a log, such as <code>try_send</code> failures that are ignored. Applying backpressure is usually better than losing data without anyone noticing.</li><li>Flag shutdown handling that differs between <code>select!</code> branches, such as one branch that cleans up a resource and another that exits without doing so.</li><li>Flag <code>Arc&lt;T&gt;</code> or <code>Mutex&lt;T&gt;</code> wrappers that nothing shares across tasks. Also flag misuse of Tokio synchronization primitives where their documented semantics differ from what the code assumes. For example, a <code>Notified</code> future from <code>Notify::notified()</code> receives <code>notify_waiters()</code> calls as soon as it is created, but isn&#39;t guaranteed to receive a <code>notify_one()</code> call until it is first polled or <code>enable()</code>d.</li><li>Flag synchronization patterns ported from Go, such as serializing every loop iteration through a rendezvous channel, when they don&#39;t fit Tokio&#39;s scheduling model.</li></ul><h3 id="error-handling" tabindex="-1">Error handling <a class="header-anchor" href="#error-handling" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Error handling&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Flag new <code>unwrap()</code>, <code>expect()</code>, or panicking indexing in non-test code where the failure is reachable from external input or configuration. Error messages should say what went wrong in plain language and, where relevant, name the configuration field involved.</p><ul><li>Untyped errors use <code>saluki_error::GenericError</code>, with context added through <code>saluki_error::ErrorContext</code> (<code>error_context</code> or <code>with_error_context</code>). Typed errors use <code>snafu</code>. In non-test code, flag new uses of <code>anyhow</code> types directly, <code>Box&lt;dyn Error&gt;</code>, or <code>String</code> as an error type.</li><li>When a recoverable condition can be handled by logging a warning and skipping the bad input, prefer that over panicking. Conditions that need someone&#39;s attention should log at <code>warn</code> or higher, not <code>info</code>.</li><li>Flag errors that are swallowed in a way that silently loses data.</li></ul><h3 id="design-and-api-conventions" tabindex="-1">Design and API conventions <a class="header-anchor" href="#design-and-api-conventions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Design and API conventions&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li>Matching the Datadog Agent&#39;s observable behavior is often required, but reproducing its internal design is not. Flag code that copies an Agent implementation detail which is a poor fit for Rust or for Saluki&#39;s architecture, unless the pull request explains why that exact behavior is required.</li><li>Conversion methods should follow the Rust API Guidelines naming conventions. <code>as_</code> is a cheap borrow, <code>to_</code> is an expensive or owned conversion, and <code>into_</code> consumes <code>self</code>. Use an <code>into_parts(self)</code>-style method when callers need owned access to the internals.</li><li>Flag hand-rolled logic that duplicates a method in the standard library or an existing workspace crate, such as a manual ASCII check instead of <code>char::is_ascii_alphanumeric</code>.</li><li>Flag constants or definitions duplicated across crates. Define them once and reference them from each place.</li><li>Flag new wrappers, traits, or layers of indirection that add nothing over the type they wrap.</li></ul><p>The following are minor. Report them as nits, ranked below correctness and performance findings:</p><ul><li>Type and function names that repeat the enclosing module or type name, such as <code>eventd::EventPriority</code> instead of <code>eventd::Priority</code>.</li><li>Small, field-less enums that don&#39;t derive <code>Copy</code>, or that allocate a <code>String</code> where a method returning <code>&amp;&#39;static str</code> would do.</li><li><code>TODO</code> comments and &quot;not yet implemented&quot; notes that don&#39;t say what is missing.</li></ul><h3 id="internal-telemetry" tabindex="-1">Internal telemetry <a class="header-anchor" href="#internal-telemetry" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Internal telemetry&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><code>agent-data-plane</code> applies the <code>adp.</code> prefix to all internal metrics at bootstrap. Flag new metric names that include the prefix themselves.</li><li>A metric must measure what its name says. For example, flag a counter named for spans that is incremented once per payload. Also check that the metric type (counter, gauge, or histogram) fits what is being measured.</li></ul><h3 id="scope" tabindex="-1">Scope <a class="header-anchor" href="#scope" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Scope&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Large refactors that a pull request merely makes possible belong in follow-up pull requests. Don&#39;t flag the lack of such a refactor as a defect. If the change introduces a hazard that it leaves unfixed, suggest filing an issue.</p><h3 id="tests" tabindex="-1">Tests <a class="header-anchor" href="#tests" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Tests&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li>Every property-based test function <strong>MUST</strong> be named with the <code>property_test_</code> prefix. The Make targets select tests by that prefix, so a misnamed property test silently runs in the wrong job.</li><li>Flag tests that cannot fail when the behavior under test regresses: no assertions, assertions only on values the test itself constructed, or a property test with no <code>prop_assert!</code>.</li><li>Flag tests that sleep to wait for background work instead of using deterministic synchronization or Tokio&#39;s paused time.</li><li>Test functions should be named as bare, descriptive sentences, without a <code>test_</code> prefix.</li><li>New behavior, including new input forms such as short-form aliases, should have unit tests that exercise it.</li><li>Flag tests that share global state, such as environment variables or process-wide singletons, without real mutual exclusion. Two separate <code>Mutex</code> instances don&#39;t serialize anything.</li><li>Flag test code that copies setup or assertions from neighboring tests when they don&#39;t apply to the behavior under test.</li></ul><h3 id="documentation-and-release-notes" tabindex="-1">Documentation and release notes <a class="header-anchor" href="#documentation-and-release-notes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Documentation and release notes&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li>All public items <strong>MUST</strong> have rustdoc. Configuration fields <strong>MUST</strong> document what they control, their default value, and their edge cases.</li><li>If a pull request changes behavior but does not update the corresponding docs, comments, or doc strings, flag it.</li><li>Reference documentation under <code>docs/reference/</code> describes how the code works today. Flag aspirational or inaccurate descriptions there.</li><li>User-visible changes to <code>agent-data-plane</code> need a release note fragment under <code>releasenotes/notes/</code>, unless the pull request carries the <code>changelog/no-changelog</code> label.</li></ul><h2 id="scoped-guidelines" tabindex="-1">Scoped guidelines <a class="header-anchor" href="#scoped-guidelines" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Scoped guidelines&quot;">​</a></h2><p>From <code>lib/datadog-agent/config/codereview_guideline.md</code>, which applies to PRs that change the configuration system:</p><h2 id="configuration-system-review-checklist" tabindex="-1">Configuration system review checklist <a class="header-anchor" href="#configuration-system-review-checklist" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Configuration system review checklist&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Apply these when the pull request touches the Datadog configuration source model.</p><ul><li>Support for Datadog schema keys is classified in <code>schema/schema_overlay.yaml</code>. Flag changes that alter which keys are supported without updating the overlay, and flag code generated from the schema and overlay that was edited by hand instead of regenerated with <code>make build-schema-overlay</code>.</li><li>Files under <code>schema/core/</code> are a vendored upstream copy of the Datadog schema. Flag any hand edit to them.</li><li>A modeled key arrives in the Datadog Agent&#39;s canonical shape, and the source model must read that shape. Flag new <code>#[serde(rename)]</code> or <code>#[serde(alias)]</code> attributes on source model fields.</li><li>Whether a key is a Datadog key or a Saluki-only key depends on whether it appears in the schema, not on its name. Flag reasoning or code that infers the class of a key from its prefix, such as <code>data_plane.</code>.</li></ul><p>From <code>lib/saluki-core/codereview_guideline.md</code>, which applies to PRs that change the core crate and data model:</p><h2 id="core-crate-review-checklist" tabindex="-1">Core crate review checklist <a class="header-anchor" href="#core-crate-review-checklist" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Core crate review checklist&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Apply these when the pull request touches <code>lib/saluki-core</code>, which holds the data model, the component and topology traits, and the runtime that every other crate builds on.</p><ul><li>The event data model under <code>src/data_model/event/</code> is Saluki&#39;s own representation of events. Flag fields or methods on event types that take or return protocol-specific types, such as OTLP or wire-format protobuf types. Translation between wire formats and the data model belongs in sources, decoders, and encoders. Recording where an event came from, for example an <code>Otlp</code> variant in an origin enum, is fine. Payload types under <code>src/data_model/payload/</code> are transport-level by design and may carry protocol types such as <code>http::Request</code>.</li><li>Flag data model accessors that clone heavy values when a reference would do. Callers that need ownership should get an <code>into_parts(self)</code>-style method or a mutable accessor instead.</li><li>Changes to public traits and types here affect every component. Flag breaking changes that the pull request doesn&#39;t call out, and flag new public API that has only one caller and could live with that caller instead.</li></ul>',46)])])}const g=t(i,[["render",r]]);export{p as __pageData,g as default};
